.TH pwb_print_array 7
.mso gproto.tmac
.SH NAME
.B pwb_print_array
\- safely displays array contents to a region
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
enable pwb
.br
source <( pwb_sources pwb_print_array )

.B pwb_print_array
.cli_prototype @array_name \
   !-t:top_line    \
   !-l:left_column \
   !-w:wide_count  \
   !-c:line_count  \
   ?!-j:c|l|r      \
   ?!-f
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Given an array whose elements constitute text lines to be
displayed, the
.B pwb_print_array
source script will print the elements within region defined
by options
.BR -t ", " -l ", " -w ", and " -c .
Each line printed will exactly fill the width of the region
and all lines in the region will be filled with content or
blank lines to ensure the entire region is filled.
.PP
The function is designed to work as part of a margin
callback function, and the full-fill guarantee ensure that
any existing content in the region will be erased.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I array_name
name of the array whose contents are to be printed.
.TP
.BI "-t " top_line
describes the top line of the region to be printed.
.TP
.BI "-l " left_column
describes the left column of the region to be printed.
.TP
.BI "-w " wide_count
describes the number of characters to print on each line.
.TP
.BI "-c " line_count
describes the number of lines in the region.
.TP
.BI "-j " justification
.BR l ", " r ", or " c
for
.BR l "eft-justified, " r "ight-justified, or " c "entered."
The default is centered.
.TP
.B "-f "
forces justified text to edge (left or right) of the defined area.
.SH NOTES
.PP
The
.B pwb_print_array
function is designed to be used with the information provided by
source script
.BR pwb_get_regions .
.B pwb_get_regions
uses
.B pwb
action
.B get_dimensions
to inform calculations of the parameters needed to print each
region using
.BR pwb_print_array .
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
.EX

enable pwb
sources <(pwb_sources pwb_print_array pwb_get_regions)

print_top()
{
   local -i index=\(dq\(Do1\(dq
   local -n pt_array=\(dq\(Do2\(dq
   local -i width=\(dq\(Do3\(dq
   local pwb_handle=\(dq\(Do5\(dq

   local -a pt_lines=(
      \(dqMy App\(dq
      \(dqRecord number \(Doindex\(dq
   )

   \fBpwb get_dimensions\fP \(dq\(Dopwb_handle\(dq
   local -a regargs=(
      -t 1
      -l \(Do(( PWB_ASSOC[margin_left] + 1 ))
      -w \(dq\(Dowidth\(dq
      -c \(dq\(Do{PWB_ASSOC[margin_top]}\(dq
   )

   \fBpwb_print_array\fP pt_lines \(dq\(Do{regargs[@]}\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
.B Alternate Global Method
.PP
The example above calculates the message area for each call to
.BR print_top .
.PP
Since the margins are typically stable, it would be reasonable to
calculate the region once for a global array that would be reused by
.B pwb_print_array
in place of the
.B regargs
array used in the example above:
.PP
.EX
set_margins PHANDLE
\fBpwb_get_regions\fP PHANDLE
declare -a HEAD_REGION=(
   -t \(dq\(Do{PWB_ASSOC[TM_TOP]}\(dq
   -l \(dq\(Do{PWB_ASSOC[TM_LEFT]}\(dq
   -w \(dq\(Do{PWB_ASSOC[TM_WIDE]}\(dq
   -c \(dq\(Do{PWB_ASSOC[TM_LINES]}\(dq
)
.EE

.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
Look at
.BR pwb_conduct_dialog " and " pwb_print_message
for other array-printing
.B pwb
sources.
